      "parsed_output": {
        "evidence": "Confirmed against the upstream fix commit b54d4e6693e0 \"SLING-12233 : Ignore resource type with invalid paths\", which adds SlingServletResolver.isInvalidPath and an early return in resolveServletInternal. Ran the POV three times via the project Docker wrapper: (1) unpatched -> \"Tests run: 1, Failures: 0\" BUILD SUCCESS, EXIT=0; (2) after temporarily applying the isInvalidPath fix -> \"Tests run: 1, Failures: 1\" BUILD FAILURE with the VULNERABLE assertion message, EXIT=1; (3) reverted the temporary patch, re-ran the full self-cleaning pov_command -> FINAL_EXIT=0. Product tree left clean (only .security-pipeline/ is untracked; isInvalidPath count back to 0).",
        "expected_vulnerable_behavior": "On the unpatched code, SlingServletResolver.resolveServlet(resolver, \"/libs/proxy/.../uploaded.servlet\") returns the attacker-registered servlet because the \"...\" path segment is neither normalized away by ResourceUtil.normalize nor rejected by isPathAllowed. The test's assertNotNull therefore passes and the command exits 0. A correct patch (isInvalidPath rejecting path segments of more than two consecutive dots, called at the top of resolveServletInternal) makes resolveServlet return null, so the assertion fails and the command exits non-zero.",
        "notes": "The canonical POV file lives at .security-pipeline/pov/PovCve202423673Test.java. Because Maven only compiles sources under src/test/java, the pov_command copies the file into src/test/java/org/apache/sling/servlets/resolver/internal/ (matching its package), runs only that test via -Dtest, then deletes the copy so no product/test tree change persists. No existing product code or tests are modified. Execution paths are unrestricted in the test config (config paths [\"/\"] -> executionPaths null), matching a common Sling deployment where isPathAllowed does not block the traversal; the \"...\" dot-segment vector is independent of execution-path configuration, which is why upstream recommends upgrading regardless of configuration.",
        "pov_command": "cp .security-pipeline/pov/PovCve202423673Test.java src/test/java/org/apache/sling/servlets/resolver/internal/PovCve202423673Test.java && mvn -B -q -DfailIfNoTests=false -Dtest=PovCve202423673Test test; rc=$?; rm -f src/test/java/org/apache/sling/servlets/resolver/internal/PovCve202423673Test.java; exit $rc",
        "pov_path": ".security-pipeline/pov/PovCve202423673Test.java",
        "regression_hint": "The POV asserts that a script/resource-type path containing a \"...\"-style segment (more than two consecutive dots) is NOT resolved to a servlet. A correct fix must reject such traversal segments before resource lookup (as in upstream SlingServletResolver.isInvalidPath called from resolveServletInternal). The test exercises the real public API SlingServletResolver.resolveServlet(ResourceResolver, String); it reuses existing test scaffolding (SlingServletResolverTestBase, HelperTestBase, MockServletResource) and is copied into src/test/java at run time, then removed. Baseline commit: 2acba56 \"baseline\".",
        "status": "pov_created",
        "summary": "Created a real proof-of-vulnerability JUnit test for CVE-2024-23673 / SLING-12233 (CWE-022 path traversal) in Apache Sling Servlets Resolver 2.10.0. The POV drives the public SlingServletResolver.resolveServlet(ResourceResolver, String) entry point with an absolute script path containing a \"...\" traversal segment (/libs/proxy/.../uploaded.servlet). ResourceUtil.normalize does not collapse the \">2 consecutive dots\" segment and isPathAllowed accepts it, so the resolver loads and returns the attacker-registered servlet. On unpatched code the servlet resolves (assertNotNull passes, exit 0); after the fix adds SlingServletResolver.isInvalidPath to reject such segments, resolveServlet returns null and the test fails (exit non-zero)."
